  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 344 PM EST Sat Nov 21 2026\n\nAfternoon GOES WV imagery and RAP analysis show a broad deep mid \nlevel trough over the Canadian Prairie and Northern Plains with a \nsharper skinnier trough extending south into the Southern Plains. \nDownstream, a ridge is noted over the Mid Atlantic Coast above a \neastward departing sfc high pressure. The resulting southwest flow \nhas maintained anomalously warm temps which have reached mid 50s to \nmid 60s this afternoon, 20-30F above normal! A cold front has made \nit to the western edge of the CWA and the resulting rain is moving \nwith it. PWATs have risen to ~1 inch ahead of the cold front. The \nfront and jet dynamics will support rainfall across the UP this \nafternoon through early Sunday afternoon when the slow moving front \nhas finally progressed east of the area. Amounts in the west are \nexpected between 0.5 and 1 inch with a corridor of 1 to 1.5 inches \nover far eastern Gogebic, southeast Ontonagon, southern Houghton, \nIron, Baraga, and northwestern Marquette counties where the frontal \nprogression is the slowest. Lower amounts are expected in the east \nbetween 0.25 and 0.5 inches. Temps steadily fall non-diurnal \ncurve through Sunday courtesy of the cold front.\n\n850 mb temps quickly fall into the negative single digits (C) on \nSunday, transitioning precip mode over to lake effect in the wake of \nthe cold front. This starts off as rain, but changes over to snow \nlate Sunday into Sunday night when the forecast begins to become a \nbit complicated. 850 mb temps continue to drop through Monday \nreaching -12C to -14C. Winds around 10-15 mph within the non-\ndecoupled boundary layer, increasing lake effect precip/clouds, and \nwarm Lake Superior will prevent the sfc temps from responding as \nquickly as the NBM has. Lows were increased to mid 20s to low 30s, \ncoldest west. Also, antecedent ground temps will play a role in \nlimiting initial accumulations. Model soundings indicate that \nwidespread thermodynamics likely would not outweigh the initial \nenvironment, but an area of much higher inversion heights and better \nmoisture through the DGZ (and above it in some guidance) inside an \narea of low level convergence in the western UP could. Monday highs \nwill be in the upper 20s to upper 30s. A shortwave trough dropping \nfrom the north could align with another period of low level \nconvergence during the day on Monday over the north-central UP where \ntemps will close to freezing most of the day. Dry air then shuts off \nlake effect Monday afternoon through Monday night from west to east. \nGiven the quick hitting nature and temperature limitations, opted to \nlower SLRs similar to the HRRR/RRFS, mainly 10:1 or lower Sunday \nnight then allow for low to mid teens on Monday. This yielded totals \nin the 1-4 inch range for the west to northwest wind snow belts by \nthe end of Monday. This is an increase from the previous forecast \npackage, but could see totals increase even more where all \ningredients line up and a Winter Weather Advisory be needed in \nfuture forecast packages. Current probabilities for accumulations \nabove 4 inches is 20-40%, highest over the terrain of Southern \nHoughton and Baraga counties.\n\nGiven anticyclonic flow, temperatures remain near seasonable through \nthe mid-week, with highs in the 30s and lows in the teens and 20s. \nAt this time, no significant precipitation chances enter the \nforecast through Thanksgiving Day.",
